 CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} -high band on a pretrained CIFAR-10 DDPM. We used google/ddpm-cifar10-32 with 250 inference steps and target class 0 (airplane). The CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} band was fixed to t ∈ [ 30 , 70 ] t\in[30,70] , while a random comparison band of comparable length was placed at t ∈ [ 180,220 ] t\in[180,220] . Each condition was evaluated over 25 seed–sample combinations and compared against both unguided sampling and full-step classifier guidance. Across guidance strengths γ ∈ { 1 , 2 , 3 , 5 } \gamma\in\{1,2,3,5\} , full classifier guidance achieved target accuracy 1.00 1.00 using all 250 intervention steps. In contrast, CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} -guided classifier guidance used only 10 intervention steps on average and still achieved target accuracy 0.96 0.96 – 1.00 1.00 . Thus, it recovers essentially the same class-control effect with only 4 % 4\% of the intervention steps (Table 1 ). The matched random-band control, despite using 11 intervention steps, remained at baseline accuracy 0.04 0.04 for all γ \gamma . This shows that the effect is not explained by the number of guided steps alone, but by their placement in the CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} instability band. The LPIPS values of CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} and random-band interventions are comparable, whereas their target accuracies differ dramatically. This separation indicates that perceptual displacement alone is not sufficient for semantic control: intervening near the detected instability band changes the branch commitment, whereas an equal-cost off-band intervention mainly induces non-semantic perturbations. In wall-clock time, CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} guidance remains close to baseline sampling, while full classifier guidance is roughly two times slower. These results support the view that CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TAD}} identifies a narrow, causally effective window for semantic control rather than merely a high-variance segment of the trajectory. Table 1: CBD TAD \mathrm{CBD_{TA